<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="resveratrol biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-84" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Arabidopsis thaliana col. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; Resveratrol (3,5,4&apos;-trihydroxystilbene) is the major polyphenol produced in a restricted number of plant genera with |FRAME:TAX-3603|, |FRAME:TAX-3817| and &lt;i&gt;Pinus&lt;/i&gt; as the prime representatives where it is formed as a general response to biotic and abiotic stress |CITS: [KINDL85]| |CITS: [SCHRODER90]| |CITS: [COMMUN03]|. Resveratrol has been recognized as a stilbene-type phytoalexin |CITS: [8421520]| |CITS: [9127691]| and represents a parent compound of a family of molecules such as glucosides (e.g. |FRAME:CPD-6958|) |CITS: [12697026]|, methylated derivatives (e.g. |FRAME:CPD-6959|) |CITS: [LANGCAKE79]| and oligomers (|FRAME:CPD-6961|, |FRAME:CPD-6960|) |CITS: [10552833]| with some of them expressing a higher fungicide toxicity than resveratrol itself. The enzyme, stilbene synthase (STS), catalyzing the formation of either resveratrol from p-coumaryl-CoA and/or pinosylvin (|FRAME:PWY-5045|) from cinnamoyl-CoA belongs to the plant specific polyketide synthases and is closely related to the chalcone synthase(s) (CHS) |CITS: [2033084]| |CITS: [8083886]|. However, and although both enzymes use the same condensation mechanism up to the tetraketide intermediate the ring closure is different leading to resveratrol via a aldol-type cyclization and decarboxylation (STS) and to naringenin chalcone by means of a Claisen-type cyclization (CHS) |CITS: [11432743]|. While chalcone synthase is present in most higher plants stilbene synthase has a much more restricted distribution in the plant kingdom. It has been demonstrated that CHS and STS share a high similarity on the DNA and protein level |CITS: [2450022]| and that the enzyme(s) activity and specificity can be altered by just a few changes on the nucleotide level |CITS: [2200709]| |CITS: [2033084]| |CITS: [1400374]| |CITS: [8083886]|. In general, although polyketide synthases (PKSs) from flowering plants are very similar in their genetic structure they differ in their substrate specificity, the number of condensation cycles and ring closure mechanism when releasing the products |CITS:[18068110]|. Stilbenes have been shown to possess a number of attributes that makes them a desirable compound class for genetic engineering. The introduction of stilbene synthase to plant species devoid of this enzymes leads to the formation of resveratrol |CITS: [2103451]| and its 3-O-glucosid (piceid) |CITS: [KOBAYASHI00]| |CITS: [8421520]|, at the same time diminishing the content of the antinutritive sinapate esters in |FRAME:TAX-3708| |CITS: [16160820]| (|FRAME:PWY-3301|) or enhancing the tolerance against the grapevine mold &lt;i&gt;Botrytis cinera&lt;/i&gt; |CITS: [11432907]|. The often observed glucosylation of resveratrol has been discussed as protective measure to prevent molecules from enzymic oxidation and therefore extending the half-life of these compounds in the cell |CITS: [12697026]|. The increase or introduction of resveratrol and its derivatives in plants is highly beneficial for the human diet since stilbenes have been demonstrated to have cardioprotective effects and anticancer properties |CITS: [15517885]| |CITS: [9127691]|. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; Resveratrol is synthesized from p-coumaroyl-CoA and 3 molecules of malonyl-CoA. The enzyme stilbene (or resveratrol) synthase is not very specific and can also accept other CoA esters, aliphatic as well as aromatic ones as primers for poylyketide synthesis including substrates not being present in plants |CITS: [12620343]| |CITS: [11432743]|. It is therefore considered as one of the major generators of carbon skeleton diversity in natural products |CITS: [Schroder99]|. A common observation for this type of reactions is the occurrence of derailment products such as bis-noryangonin and p-coumaroyltriacetic acid lactone. The formation of bis-noryangonin has been detected in most stilbene synthase catalyzed reactions |CITS: [12620343]| |CITS: [11432743]| and has been described as an artificial bye-product of the chalcone/stilbene synthase reaction after the addition of 2 malonyl-CoA |CITS: [1155951]|. However, the enzyme responsible for the catalysis of bisnoryangonin, i.e. styrylpyrone synthase has been partially purified from |FRAME:TAX-3258| and demonstrated to be different from chalcone oder stilbene synthase. The enzyme also accepts with lower efficiency caffeoyl-CoA which leads to the formation of hispidin (|FRAME:CPD-6989|) representing another styrylpyrone detected in this more primitive gametophyte |CITS: [BECKERT97]|. The formation of p-coumaroyltriacetic acid lactone is catalyzed by a novel enzyme distinct from stilbene synthase |CITS: [10469148]|. The enzyme converts p-coumaryl-CoA and 3 molecules of malonyl-CoA to a novel pyrone, a linear tetraketide which has been elongated but not cyclized, i.e. p-coumaroyltriacetate. The final cyclization to p-coumaryltriacetic acid lactone occurs spontaneously. The polyketide type III synthase (PKS III) from &lt;i&gt;Polygonum cuspidatum&lt;/i&gt; represents yet another type in this superfamily. In contrast to almost all PKS III proteins which have 1 intron in their gene structure |CITS:[Schroder97]|, the PKS III encoded by PKS2 from &lt;i&gt;Polygonum&lt;/i&gt; has 3 introns. Moreover, the highly conserved |FRAME:PHE| &apos;gatekeeper&apos; residues Phe215 and Phe265 in the active site of PKS IIIs were replaced by |FRAME:LEU| and |FRAME:CYS| in PKS2. Interestingly, the PKS2 encoded protein shows a different substrate specificity in dependence on the pH conditions, at physiological normal pH (6.5-8.5) the recombinant enzyme acted as p-coumaryltriacetic lactone synthase (CTAS) forming predominantly |FRAME:CPD-6954| and |FRAME:CPD-6955| (this pathway). In contrast to that, at a higher pH of 9.0-9.5, the PKS2 mainly catalyzed the formation of |FRAME:CPD-8636|| after only one round of condensation, hence acting as benzalacetone synthase (BAS) (see |FRAME:PWY-5393|) |CITS:[18998157]|.</Description>
